 "transcript": "Nah, calling the culling game a tournament is crazy. This is basically Fortnite, except there's no winner, and even camping can get you killed. And I don't know about you, but after Shibuya, JJK went from fighting curses to\u2026 hold up. Who signed everybody up for Squid Game? Remember when Kenjaku absorbed Mahito? Well, he used Mahito's idle transfiguration to activate people he'd been preparing for years. Some ordinary humans suddenly awakened cursed techniques. Others became vessels for ancient sorcerers who had been turned into cursed objects centuries ago. Think Yuji and Sukuna. Same basic idea of an old sorcerer coming back through a modern human body. Now, Kenjaku divides Japan into 10 colonies, surrounded by barriers. Think of each colony as its own battle royale lobby. Except, the kills aren't there to decide a winner. They're generating cursed energy for Kenjaku's real goal. Merging humanity with Tengen. Okay, so how do you actually play this thing? There are eight original rules, but here's what makes them dangerous. If Kenjaku awakened you, you've got 19 days to declare participation. Refuse, and your cursed technique gets removed, which can kill you. And if you weren't selected but walk into a colony anyway\u2026 Congratulations, you're a player now! You even get Kogane, this little Shikigami that basically works like your in -game menu. It explains rules, keeps track of points, and announces updates. But here's the disturbing part. The original way to earn points is by killing other players. Kill a sorcerer, generally five points. A non -sorcerer, one. Get a hundred points and you can spend them to propose a new rule. And that's where I got lost. Why not just make a rule that says everybody can go home? Because you can't delete existing rules, and the Game Master can reject anything that seriously interferes with the game's continuation. And remember what I said about camping? If your score doesn't change for 19 days, your cursed technique can get removed. Bro, even the storm has a rulebook. You can't win by being the last person alive, and you can't just wait for the match to end. The fighting is the whole point. Every battle helps fuel Kenjaku's ritual. You're not competing for a victory, you're basically another Kog in his machine. And that's why Yuji and Megumi get involved. Megumi's sister, Tsumiki, is caught in the game, and they're trying to save her without murdering innocent people. So instead of trying to beat the game, they start looking for ways around its rules. With Higuruma's help, they get a rule added that allows players to transfer points, meaning you can keep your score changing without killing anyone. That's one loophole, but it doesn't end the ritual. Now imagine you're standing in front of Kogane with a hundred points. You can't simply cancel the game, and your new rule still has to be accepted. What would you propose to get everybody out?",
